explanation:
The definition first views the scalar kernel K as an operator-valued kernel on the one-dimensional Hilbert space 𝕜: the entry at (a, b) is multiplication by K a b. It then uses Mathlib's `RKHS.OfKernel` completion construction and takes the kernel vector `RKHS.kerFun _ a`, evaluated at the scalar 1. This is exactly the reproducing-kernel vector K(·, a) in the RKHS of K.

The library proves the expected properties:
- the Kolmogorov identity `⟪Φ a, Φ b⟫_𝕜 = K a b`, which uses Mathlib's convention that the inner product is conjugate-linear in the first argument;
- density of the linear span of the vectors Φ a, i.e. minimality of the decomposition;
- a universal isometry into any other realization of K.

The setting is already the natural one: 𝕜 is any `RCLike` field, α is an arbitrary index type, and `Matrix.PosSemidef K` on α × α matrices is the standard definition of a positive-semidefinite kernel. Mathlib only has the operator-valued `RKHS.kerFun`, which this definition specializes to the scalar case. The scalar Kolmogorov feature map does not appear in Mathlib as a separate declaration, so there is no Mathlib counterpart to cite.
